# Generic Functional Tests

Follow the `functional-test-generation` workflow first. Use this skill when no framework-specific skill matches the repository's tests.

## Identify the framework from the repository
- Find the test framework from the build or dependency files and from the existing test files. Record the evidence (file and dependency name).
- Read at least two existing functional tests completely. Learn from them:
  - How the system under test is reached (HTTP client, browser driver, message client, CLI, in-process client)
  - How configuration and environment values are loaded
  - How setup and teardown are done
  - How assertions are written
  - How tests are named, tagged, and organized
- Find the framework's documentation in the repository (README, docs folder, comments) if the conventions are unclear.

## Writing tests
- Write the new tests in exactly the same style, structure, and libraries as the existing tests.
- Use only APIs and functions you have seen in the existing tests or in the framework's code in the repository. Do not use functions you have not seen used.
- Apply every rule in `functional-test-generation`: one scenario per test, own test data, unique values, condition-based waiting, full assertions, no hardcoded secrets or URLs, and no order dependence.

## No existing functional tests
If the repository has no functional tests at all:
- Do not choose and add a new framework. Record every scenario as `BLOCKED` with the reason "no functional test framework in repository".
- List in `details` the test-related dependencies the repository already declares, so a person can decide which framework to adopt.

## Run
- Use the command from the build files, package scripts, CI pipeline files, or README. Record the exact command in the report.
